@* The bot check's two fields (NFR-04, InquiryFormGuard). Goes inside an EditForm whose page binds an
   InquiryFormGuardInput property called Name under the same FormName, so these post as Name.Stamp and
   Name.Extra and bind to it.

   The trap is hidden with display:none from site.css — not the hidden attribute or type=hidden, which
   a form-filling bot reads and skips, and not an off-screen trick, which leaves it focusable. A
   display:none field is out of reach of the keyboard, screen readers and browser autofill, so a person
   never sends a value in it. The value is always rendered empty, including on a redraw. *@
<input type="hidden" name="@($"{Name}.Stamp")" value="@Stamp" />
<div class="form-trap">
    <label for="@($"{Name}-extra")">Lämna det här fältet tomt</label>
    <input type="text" id="@($"{Name}-extra")" name="@($"{Name}.Extra")" value="" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The name of the page's bound <c>InquiryFormGuardInput</c> property.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The stamp to post, from <c>InquiryFormGuard.StampFor</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Stamp { get; set; } = "";
}
